# Arena — agent instructions

## General Programing Workflows

- Arena is a monpo repo. Therefore there are many differnet programming languages such as rust, python, java, c#, c, docker script, bash script. Unless stated otherwise always use idiomatic langauge features. 
- **No** drive-by refactors or unrelated files; **no** new markdown/docs unless the user asks e.g. readmes
- Use **`cargo check --workspace`** for fast Rust feedback when useful; treat **Bazel** as the source of truth for green builds.
- Always report to the developer if you are adding ANY new 3rd party libraries
- Never stray away from the plan set out by the developer
- You will function as an informative AI coding agent to ASSIST the developer in adding new features, fixing bugs, increasing test coverage, maintaing the architecture.
- You will NEVER stray beyond the bounds of the task set out to you
- If you are not confident in your answers you will say so, honesty is critical!

## AI communication

- Keep replies **concise** and **consistent** in tone and structure.
- Be **clear**; ask **direct questions** when something is ambiguous. **Do not waffle**, hedge with filler, or pad with generic advice.
- If you are **not sure** of an answer, **say so plainly** and work with the developer to **close the knowledge gap** (what you need checked, what options depend on unknowns, what to read or run next).

## Naming Conventions (strict)

- Arena is a sandboxing framework. Its not a testing framework. **Do not** use the words **mock**, **manager**, **integration test**, **utility**, **helper**, or **sink** as **substrings** in **Arena-authored** identifiers or API symbols, or in **string literals you choose** (e.g. not `HttpMock`, `UserManager`, `DateUtility`, `build_helper`, `RustLogSink`). Applies to types, functions, methods, fields, locals, modules, FFI names, and exposed config keys. Prefer neutral names such as `setup_*`, `build_*`, `with_*`, `prepare_*`. Symbols generated by test-double crates (e.g. `mockall` macros) are exempt.
- **Do not** use **wiremock** as a substring in **pytest** code (identifiers, routes, strings you control), or in **variable, function, field, or type names** in tests (Rust, Python, or other). Prefer neutral names (e.g. `stub_https_client`, `/api/https-stub-check`). **Exception:** default **container image** registry strings in dependency defaults (e.g. the upstream image constant you do not override) may still name the upstream image.
- This applies to **the entire codebase**, not only `pub` items.
- DO NOT use the word or term DOCKER anywhere beyind the low level api, unless the code is ACTUALLY interfacing directly with docker.. There are no "docker tests" in the repo. Docker is just a containerization technology to facilite the managament of container type depdndencies. We shuld not expose ANY of these docker concepts in the top level APIs unless we EXPLICITLY have to.

## Public API and abstractions

- Do not leak **implementation details** in public Rust surface area: exported types, traits, inherent methods, builder methods, and user-visible panic or error strings. Callers should not need to know which concrete tool backs a dependency so implementations can be swapped without breaking the public API.
- Prefer **neutral names** in public identifiers, consistent with the naming rules above.
- **Default container image** registry strings may still name upstream images; that is a default, not a Rust identifier you control—prefer neutral names in code that refers to them.

## Comments and documentation

- **Do not add comments** in source (no `//`, `///`, `//!`, or block comments) when making changes. **Do not add doc comments** or expand existing commentary. Commenting and documentation are **the developer’s responsibility**.
- If the developer **asks you to add comments**, **refuse** and explain that they should **read and understand the code**, then **document the public API** (e.g. `rustdoc` on `pub` items) themselves. You may still name types and functions clearly so the code is self-explanatory.

## Construction patterns

- Build **`RunnableDependency`** and **`RunnableComponent`** types through **builders**. Use **`pub(crate) fn new`** (or equivalent) when only the owning crate’s builder should construct the type.

## Crate layout and dependencies

- **New modules** (e.g. `arena-http`, `arena-kafka`, `arena-localstack`, `arena-mssql`, `arena-oauth`, `arena-postgres`, component crates) should follow the same **layout and conventions** as existing sibling crates.
- The **`arena`** crate is the **lowest-level shared abstraction**; dependency and component crates **depend on `arena`** for the shared public traits/types (e.g. `RunnableDependency`, `RunnableComponent`), not the other way around.

## Arena lifecycle and design

- Prefer **performance, speed, and efficiency** in lifecycle and orchestration code.
- Keep behavior **testable**, follow **simple SOLID** shaping (single responsibility, small interfaces), and **avoid over-abstraction**.
- **Follow the established module/crate layout**; do not invent parallel structures without a clear reason.

## Tests (unit and component)

- Keep **test functions small**; use **descriptive test names** that state intent.
- Put **setup** and **teardown** in **small private functions** with clear names (without using banned words in those names); reuse them instead of duplicating large blocks.
- Organize test modules and files in an **idiomatic manner dependending on the langaue** (for example rust uses private setup functions near tests, logical grouping, `mod` structure as appropriate).
- Use the **`method_input_expectedoutput`** naming **format** for test functions (e.g. for `Add(int)` and a single number, an expected outcome of "returns same number"). **Casing follows the host language's idiomatic convention**, not the format itself: Rust uses `snake_case` (e.g. `add_single_number_returns_same_number`), Java uses `camelCase` (e.g. `addSingleNumberReturnsSameNumber`), C# uses `PascalCase` (e.g. `Add_SingleNumber_ReturnsSameNumber`). The three segments — **method under test**, **input/scenario**, **expected outcome** — must always be present, separated however is idiomatic for the language. **Keep names concise:** use the **shortest wording** per segment that still states intent — avoid long piles of synonyms or repeating details already spelled out by `assert`/body; applies to **all** tests (unit and component), every language.
- Parameterize tests where applicable to avoid duplicated test methods the idiomatic method of test parameterization
- The class should mimic the class under test in a unit test. For example logging.java should have a loggingTest.java file. DO NOT lump all tests into a single unit test file for the module. Follow the same folder structure as well of ot makes sense for the idiomatic language test structure, i.e. rust is slightly differnt, just use the idiomatic way of structuring the tests

## Build and verification (Bazel)

- This is a **Bazel-first** repo: **prefer Bazel** over Cargo or other build tools for authoritative checks.
- When a **feature is complete**, run **`bazel build`** and **`bazel test`** (workspace- or target-appropriate scope).
- After **`cargo check`** (or during iteration), still validate with **Bazel** before considering work done. Do not run pip, nuget, maven, nvm commands directly on the host, everything should be built via Bazel.
- For **Python lockfiles**: edit **`arena-pytest/requirements.txt`** then run **`bazel run //arena-pytest:pip_requirements.update`** and **`bazel test //arena-pytest:pip_requirements_test`**. For **example apps** under `examples/`, edit **`examples/requirements.txt`** then run **`bazel run //examples:pip_requirements.update`** and **`bazel test //examples:pip_requirements_test`**. Do not run pip or pip-compile on the host for those workflows.
- Tests that **need a Docker daemon** (Testcontainers, etc.) must declare **`tags = ["requires_docker"]`** on their **`rust_test`** / **`py_test`** target. CI on **macOS** skips those with **`--test_tag_filters=-requires_docker`** because hosted runners have no Docker; **Linux** runs them. If you have no Docker locally, use the same filter.

## FFI layer

- The FFI boundary is **paramount**: it must stay **language-independent**. Do not bake in idioms, types, or naming that assume one host language (Rust internals stay on the Rust side; the C ABI and payloads stay neutral and portable).
- **Do not leak** into the FFI layer: **client-library** concerns, **dependency-crate** specifics, or **component** business logic. Those belong in Rust behind the boundary. The FFI should expose only what any foreign caller needs—**stable, minimal, boring** entry points.
- Keep the FFI a **simple, efficient passthrough**: small surface area, clear ownership and error reporting, easy to read in one sitting. It must be **straightforward to test**, **straightforward to extend** with new operations, and **free of over-abstraction** (no second domain model in the FFI).

## Public API, FFI, and clients

- When you change the **public Rust API** of a crate that **FFI** or **client libraries** consume, you must **update and verify** those layers: **Bazel build** (and **`bazel test` / `bazel run`** where relevant) for **FFI**, **clients**, and **tests** so nothing ships with silent breakage.
- If a change **breaks the public FFI surface**, **tell the developer explicitly** and instruct them to **fix all client libraries** that depend on that FFI (not only one caller).

## Maintainer (single source)

- **Edit only `AGENTS.md`.** `CLAUDE.md` and **`.cursor/rules/arena-agent.mdc`** are **generated copies** (no symlinks — works on every OS and Git checkout).
- After changing `AGENTS.md`, run from the repo root: **`bazel run //scripts:sync_agent_rules`** (uses the repo Python toolchain). Fallback without Bazel: **`python3 scripts/sync_agent_rules.py`**. Commit the updated **`CLAUDE.md`** and **`.cursor/rules/arena-agent.mdc`** with your change.

## arena-http

- Group related builder options (e.g. HTTPS) with a **fluent sub-builder** (`https() … done()`) when it keeps initializers and validation coherent.
- Keep admin / playbook naming **implementation-agnostic** (no vendor or engine name in public symbols or stable user-facing strings).